Why This Works Across Pods
Distributed Nature: When you use Redis as the cache store, all 4 pods connect to the same Redis instance. When Pod A caches getOwnerByType("admin"), the result is stored in Redis. When Pod B later calls the same method, Spring checks Redis first and finds the cached value.

Automatic Synchronization: You don't need custom pub/sub or messaging because the shared Redis instance IS the source of truth. Any pod can read what any other pod cached.

Eviction Propagation: When you call @CacheEvict on any pod, it removes the key from Redis. All other pods immediately see it's gone on their next read operation.

Important Considerations
Avoid allEntries = true with Redis
Using @CacheEvict(allEntries = true) with Redis executes a KEYS * command, which can severely impact performance on large caches. Instead, evict specific keys:

java
// Avoid this
@CacheEvict(value = "ownerCache", allEntries = true)

// Do this instead
@CacheEvict(value = "ownerCache", key = "#type")
TTL as a Safety Net
Always set reasonable TTLs. Even if something goes wrong with explicit eviction, Redis will automatically expire stale data:

Short TTL (5 minutes) for frequently changing data

Longer TTL (1 hour) for relatively static data

Serialization Format
Use GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer for values so your Locker class (which implements Serializable) is properly stored and retrieved across pods.

For High-Volume Scenarios
If you need even lower latency, consider a two-tier approach:

L1: Caffeine (local to each pod) for ultra-fast reads

L2: Redis (shared across pods) for consistency

With this pattern, you'd use Redis Pub/Sub to broadcast invalidation messages so all pods know to update their local caches. However, for most applications, a single Redis tier works perfectly.

Deployment in Kubernetes
Your Redis instance should be deployed separately from your application pods. All 4 replicas should have the same Redis connection configuration pointing to that central Redis service. This is typically handled through environment variables or ConfigMaps in your Kubernetes deployment.

